WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Identity is often the first containment priority in cloud incidents because cloud control

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.820
is heavily identity-driven.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.080
A compromised user account, service account, automation identity, token,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.420
or access key may allow changes across many services.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:21.080
If the identity has broad permissions, the incident can expand quickly into data access,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.140
configuration changes, resource creation, logging changes,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.840
or lateral movement across environments.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.180
Responders should review recent identity activity, including logins, source context,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.480
MFA behavior, privilege changes, role assumptions, key creation, token use,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.220
group membership changes, and access to sensitive resources.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.520
The goal is to determine whether the identity was used in a way that matches normal

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:48.620
behavior,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.040
an approved automation path, or suspicious activity.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.980
The same evidence also informs containment scope.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.800
Containment may include disabling or restricting affected identities,

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.060
rotating credentials, revoking sessions where supported, removing unauthorized keys,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.200
tightening policies, or temporarily limiting high-risk permissions.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.140
These actions should be documented and coordinated because identity changes can interrupt

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.800
production services,

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.300
automation, deployments, or integrations.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.240
The safest containment is specific enough to stop misuse without causing unnecessary

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.420
outages.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.080
Leaked access keys deserve urgent attention because they may allow API access from outside

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.780
normal user sessions depending on permissions.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.440
Emergency credential rotation should be paired with evidence review.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.300
If a key is deleted before its use is understood,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.560
responders may lose context about what systems depended on it and what activity occurred.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.100
The answer is not to delay containment forever,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.080
but to preserve what can be preserved before major changes when practical.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.540
Recovery should move toward least privilege.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.160
After the immediate incident, review why the identity had the permissions it had,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.220
whether MFA coverage was sufficient, whether service accounts were overbroad,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.640
and whether detection covered key creation and privilege changes.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.940
Identity incidents are often symptoms of deeper access governance gaps.
